WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:06.539
Hello everyone, welcome to the Lang Focus Channel and my name is Paul. Today's topic is Moroccan

00:00:06.670 --> 00:00:10.260
Darija, Morocco's Arabic dialect or is it?

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:18.209
Moroccan Darija is normally considered to belong to the Meghrebi Arabic dialect group which includes not only Moroccan Arabic but also

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:21.420
Algerian, Tunisian and Hassaniyan Arabic.

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:28.260
Moroccan Darija is also known as the Arabic dialect which is the most difficult for speakers of other dialects to understand.

00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:32.480
And the further you travel east away from Morocco the more difficult it becomes.

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:37.540
Some Arabic speakers say that hearing Moroccan Darija is like hearing a different language,

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:44.060
and some Moroccans insists that Darija is actually a different language because it's influenced by the Amazigh language

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:50.420
Still other Moroccans insist that Darija is indeed Arabic and is closely connected with Classical Arabic

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:57.440
In the 7th century CE, the Islamic conquest of the Maghreb region brought both Islam and the Arabic language to Morocco

00:00:57.730 --> 00:01:01.949
But before that Morocco's population consisted mostly of Berber people

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:05.500
The term Berber actually refers to Amazigh people,

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:09.240
a North African ethnic group that speaks Amazigh languages.

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:13.340
When these Amazigh speakers learned Arabic, the language of the new rulers,

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:17.960
Their native Amazigh languages naturally influenced the way they spoke Arabic.

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:20.980
And this is the source of some of Darija's unique features.

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:24.200
The Iberian Peninsula also came under Muslim control.

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:30.540
This resulted in significant interaction between Morocco and Spanish and Portuguese speakers from the nearby peninsula.

00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:36.200
In the 19th century, French economic interest in Morocco brought French influence to the country

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:41.020
and in 1912 Morocco was divided into French and Spanish Protectorates.

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:44.460
The Spanish Protectorate occupied a strip of the northern coast,

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:48.300
as well as a strip of land called Cape Juby next to the Spanish Sahara.

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:51.760
The French protectorate occupied all the land in between.

00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:55.320
This colonial presence had an influence on Moroccan Darija,

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:58.660
in particular in the form of French and Spanish loanwords

00:01:58.660 --> 00:02:03.700
These protectorates came to an end in 1956 when Morocco gained independence

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:07.080
Western Sahara remained a Spanish protectorate until

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:09.620
1975 when it was annexed by Morocco.

00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:11.020
Upon independence

00:02:11.020 --> 00:02:13.540
Morocco's official language became Arabic.

00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:17.130
And in 2011, Amazigh was also made an official language

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:22.640
But French continues to be a prestige language alongside modern Standard Arabic until this day.

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:24.420
Many Moroccans are fluent in French,

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:31.260
especially in the major city and it's commonly used in business and in government and it's the main language of university instruction.

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:35.320
This has resulted in the continuous influence of French on Moroccan Darija,

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:40.020
in particular, academic vocabulary and words related to modern innovations.

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:42.660
And there's also an element of code switching of

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:46.820
impulsively including French words and expressions in Darija off-the-cuff.

00:02:46.820 --> 00:02:49.920
Currently in Morocco about 65% of the population

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:52.200
Speaks Darija as a first language.

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:55.820
Around 30% speaks in Amazigh dialect as a first language.

00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:59.400
But the vast majority of them speak Darija as a second language as well.

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:02.180
Modern Standard Arabic and French are learned at school

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:07.260
Modern Standard Arabic is limited to certain formal situations media and writing

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:09.860
whereas Darija is used for daily communication

00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:11.791
rather than modern Standard Arabic.

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:14.180
Now, let's take a look at Moroccan. Darija

00:03:14.180 --> 00:03:19.780
Moroccan Darija differs from modern Standard Arabic and from other Arabic dialects in a number of ways

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:21.140
Vocabulary

00:03:21.140 --> 00:03:25.180
As I mentioned before, there are many French loanwords in Moroccan Darija

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:30.360
For example the word for table "Tabla", which comes from French "table",

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:33.480
or as in Standard Arabic. It's Taawila

00:03:33.580 --> 00:03:34.640
"hospital"

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:37.280
"sbitar" from French "hôpital",

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:42.440
though I suspect it shares a double etymology with Spanish because of the "s" in the word.

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:44.620
In Spanish, it's "hospital".

00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:47.640
In Standard Arabic, it's "mustashfaa"

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:48.880
"apartment"

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:51.520
"barTmah" from French "appartement",

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:54.460
In Standard Arabic, it's "shaqa"

00:03:54.460 --> 00:03:57.600
To a lesser extent there is also Spanish vocabulary.

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:01.460
The word for "kitchen", "kuziina", from Spanish "cocina".

00:04:01.460 --> 00:04:04.160
In Standard Arabic, it's "maTbakh"

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:08.060
"week", "simana" from Spanish "semana".

00:04:08.060 --> 00:04:10.740
In Standard Arabic, it's "usbuu3"

00:04:11.620 --> 00:04:13.640
Some words come from Amazigh.

00:04:13.640 --> 00:04:15.980
The word meaning how much, "shHal"

00:04:16.180 --> 00:04:19.100
"shHal fi 3omrak?" this means "how old are you".

00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:21.540
In Standard Arabic, It's "kam".

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:26.440
The word for "tea", "atay". The Arabic word is "shay"

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:30.980
And some words are of Arabic origin, but have changed or are used differently.

00:04:30.980 --> 00:04:34.080
The Moroccan word for "a lot" is "bzzaf".

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:37.540
This is the equivalent of "kathiir" in standard Arabic

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:42.900
It comes from Classical Arabic "bijuzaf", which means "a lot" or "without measure".

00:04:42.980 --> 00:04:49.440
The Darija word for "who" is "shkun". This probably comes from the classical Arabic. I "Aya shay ykunn",

00:04:49.820 --> 00:04:55.380
which means "anything is" or "whichever is", but its meaning has evolved to mean "who".

00:04:55.460 --> 00:05:01.360
The Darija word for the number two is "juj", whereas in Standard Arabic, it's "ithnaan".

00:05:01.540 --> 00:05:05.880
"Juj" comes from the Arabic word "zawj", meaning "pair" or "couple"

00:05:05.960 --> 00:05:06.960
Pronunciation

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:13.540
One of the most important features of Moroccan Darija and the one that makes it the most difficult for speakers of other dialects to

00:05:13.650 --> 00:05:15.880
understand is vowel reduction.

00:05:15.880 --> 00:05:19.680
Short vowels are often reduced to a schwa sound or dropped entirely

00:05:19.740 --> 00:05:24.900
resulting in new consonant clusters and sometimes in syllables that consists of only consonants

00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:29.000
This is because of the influence of a Amazigh phonology on Darija.

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:36.000
For example, "balad" meaning "country" or "city" in Moroccan Darija. It's pronounced "bled".

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:41.780
Another example, "katab". This is the past tense verb stem of the verb meaning "to write".

00:05:41.780 --> 00:05:45.340
But in Moroccan pronunciation, it's like this: "ktib".

00:05:45.620 --> 00:05:50.000
"huna" means "here", but in Morocco it's "h'naa".

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:51.140
In all of these examples,

00:05:51.140 --> 00:05:54.040
the Arabic word has stress on the first syllable

00:05:54.040 --> 00:05:57.120
but in the Darija word, the stress moves to the final vowel

00:05:57.160 --> 00:05:59.680
and the earlier vowel is reduced or dropped.

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:03.220
This tendency becomes a bit more important with multiple words.

00:06:03.320 --> 00:06:08.900
"anta taktubu" This means "you write" but in Morocco, it's pronounced "nta ktktb"

00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:11.500
"anta taktubu" - "nta ktktb"

00:06:11.789 --> 00:06:12.620
Grammar

00:06:12.620 --> 00:06:17.880
I would say that Moroccan Darija grammar is not radically different from other Arabic dialects,

00:06:17.920 --> 00:06:23.120
which share a lot in common with each other and which differ from modern Standard Arabic in similar ways

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:28.340
But the influence of Amazigh grammar is indeed obvious in a number of different structures

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:31.760
One example is the lack of a dual form in Darija.

00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:37.880
In Standard Arabic, there are singular, plural and dual forms. Dual being used when there are two of something.

00:06:37.880 --> 00:06:40.780
In Standard Arabic, we have "kitaabaan",

00:06:40.780 --> 00:06:44.400
but in Darija, it's "juj ktuba".

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:47.520
Another example is the passive form in Moroccan Darija.

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:51.800
In Standard Arabic, the passive is formed by changing the vowels of the verb.

00:06:51.800 --> 00:06:56.780
"katab al-kitaab" means "he wrote the book" -&gt; "kutiba al-kitaab"

00:06:56.920 --> 00:07:02.280
In Moroccan Darija, you formed the passive by simply inserting "t" before an active verb.

00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:05.600
"ktb Iktab" means "he wrote the book" and

00:07:05.660 --> 00:07:09.180
"I ktab tktb" means the book was written

00:07:09.260 --> 00:07:16.880
Another example is a pattern of forming abstract nouns from other nouns by prefixing "ta" and adding "t" as a suffix.

00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:19.500
"kəddab" means "liar" and

00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:22.520
"takəddabət" means "habitual lying".

00:07:22.520 --> 00:07:24.540
This same pattern is found in Amazigh.

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:32.720
This particular word is not used in all local dialects, but there are lots of other similarly formed words often related to occupations

00:07:32.960 --> 00:07:37.840
Also, the reflexive in Darija directly mirrors the Amazigh reflexive.

00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:39.500
"Ayoub shaf rasu flmraya"

00:07:39.500 --> 00:07:42.160
This means "Ayoub saw himself in the mirror"

00:07:42.160 --> 00:07:45.720
The way "himself" is expressed literally means "his head".

00:07:45.980 --> 00:07:48.620
In Standard Arabic, the sentence would be

00:07:48.620 --> 00:07:51.420
"ra'a Ayoub nasfu fi l-mar'at".

00:07:51.440 --> 00:07:55.880
In Standard Arabic, a different word "nafs" is used instead of "ras".

00:07:55.880 --> 00:08:00.400
One extra thing to notice here is that the Standard Arabic sentence is VSO

00:08:00.410 --> 00:08:02.500
The verb comes at the beginning.

00:08:02.500 --> 00:08:07.220
But the Darija sentence is SVO with the subject before the verb

00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:11.860
Now, let's look at a few Moroccan Darija sentences and break them down

00:08:12.060 --> 00:08:15.700
This sentence means "I ride the bus to school every day".

00:08:15.700 --> 00:08:18.300
"kanrakab ttobis lmdrasa kul nhar"

00:08:18.460 --> 00:08:23.260
word for word is, "I ride the bus the school every day".

00:08:23.260 --> 00:08:25.440
In Standard Arabic, it would be

00:08:25.440 --> 00:08:28.660
"ana arkab al-Hafila ila al-madrasa kulu yawm."

00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:32.860
First, notice that the Standard Arabic sentence has a subject pronoun,

00:08:32.860 --> 00:08:34.780
while the Darija just sentence doesn't

00:08:34.860 --> 00:08:37.540
Actually, it's optional in Standard Arabic as well.

00:08:37.540 --> 00:08:42.340
So with that optional pronoun aside, notice that the first word of the sentence is different

00:08:42.340 --> 00:08:48.740
The Moroccan word has a prefix "ka" to indicate present tense, while the Standard Arabic sentence does not

00:08:48.840 --> 00:08:53.360
Also, the standard Arabic word has the first person singular prefix "a",

00:08:53.440 --> 00:08:59.840
while the Moroccan word has "n". In Standard Arabic the prefix "n" would indicate first person plural.

00:09:00.020 --> 00:09:04.240
You may have also noticed that the vowel patterns of these two words are different

00:09:04.340 --> 00:09:12.480
Next, notice this word "tobis" which comes from the French word "autobus". The Standard Arabic word is "Hafila"

00:09:12.620 --> 00:09:18.240
Both before "tobis" and before the next word "mdrasa" we see the definite article.

00:09:18.280 --> 00:09:22.760
In Standard Arabic, it's "al" but in Darija it's reduced to "l".

00:09:22.960 --> 00:09:27.140
In "mdrasa", notice that the vowel pattern and stress are different,

00:09:27.140 --> 00:09:31.820
with the Darija word losing its first vowel or it becomes a very short schwa sound

00:09:31.820 --> 00:09:36.480
Next, we see "kul nhar" and in Standard Arabic we see "kulu yawn"

00:09:36.500 --> 00:09:42.640
"nhar" and "yawn" are both Standard Arabic words and both exist in Darija with slightly different pronunciation.

00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:45.620
This is simply a matter of dialectal word choice.

00:09:45.620 --> 00:09:48.740
You could also say "kul yuum" in Moroccan Darija.

00:09:49.380 --> 00:09:53.820
Another sentence. This means "I don't have any cats, but I have nine fish."

00:09:53.820 --> 00:09:57.180
"ma3andish shi mush, wa lakin 3andi tes3a dial hutat."

00:09:57.180 --> 00:10:04.220
Word for word, it's "not I have negative any cat, but I have nine of fish

00:10:04.860 --> 00:10:07.180
In Standard Arabic, it would be

00:10:07.180 --> 00:10:11.120
"Laysa 3andi 'aya qaTaT, wa lakin 3andi tis3 'asmaak."

00:10:11.120 --> 00:10:14.680
Your first impression might be that these two sentences are quite different

00:10:14.720 --> 00:10:17.440
First the way of expressing the negative is different.

00:10:17.440 --> 00:10:24.200
"3andi" is actually not a verb but a preposition meaning "with" plus the pronominal suffix "i" meaning me.

00:10:24.200 --> 00:10:28.180
In Standard Arabic you make this sentence negative using "Laysa",

00:10:28.180 --> 00:10:33.160
while in Moroccan Darija you make it negative by prefixing "ma" and suffixing "sh".

00:10:33.160 --> 00:10:36.640
This is a double negative like in French. "No" plus "pas".

00:10:36.660 --> 00:10:44.080
This is not exclusively Moroccan and a similar form is used generally in North African dialects and sometimes in Palestinian as well

00:10:44.080 --> 00:10:48.560
"shi" means thing but is used with the negative to mean "any" or "anything".

00:10:48.560 --> 00:10:51.920
The Standard Arabic equivalent is "'aya".

00:10:52.020 --> 00:10:54.920
Next we see that the word for "cat" is different.

00:10:54.920 --> 00:10:59.560
"mush" is the Darija word for "cat" and was borrowed from Amazigh.

00:10:59.720 --> 00:11:03.920
The word for "9" is "tes3a", which is similar to the standard Arabic word

00:11:03.960 --> 00:11:08.520
There's also another word for "9" which is often used in Morocco "ts3ud".

00:11:08.520 --> 00:11:10.900
This depends on where you are in Morocco

00:11:10.920 --> 00:11:16.280
Next in Darija, we see "dial" meaning "of" this is not present in Standard Arabic

00:11:16.280 --> 00:11:20.660
Then we have the word "hutat" which is the Darija word for "fish".

00:11:20.720 --> 00:11:25.880
In Standard Arabic and in most other dialects it would be "'asmaak" or something similar

00:11:26.200 --> 00:11:29.060
Another sentence. "waash bghiti kas d l-ma?"

00:11:29.060 --> 00:11:31.220
This means "do you want a glass of water?"

00:11:31.420 --> 00:11:36.300
Word for word it's "question marker", "you want" "glass" "of" "the" "water".

00:11:36.640 --> 00:11:41.040
In Standard Arabic it would be "hal turiid kas min al-ma'".

00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:45.420
The first word here "waash" is used as a marker foryes-or-no questions.

00:11:45.800 --> 00:11:48.600
In Standard Arabic "hal" or "a" are used .

00:11:48.900 --> 00:11:53.620
The next word "bghiti" is actually the past tense form of a verb meaning "to like"

00:11:53.620 --> 00:11:56.980
but in the past tense form like this it's similar to "would like".

00:11:56.980 --> 00:11:59.080
In the present tense it means "like".

00:11:59.080 --> 00:12:04.760
This form with the suffix "t" is the second person singular form for both masculine and feminine in

00:12:04.760 --> 00:12:09.540
In Standard Arabic, there are separate forms for masculine singular and feminine singular.

00:12:09.580 --> 00:12:12.040
But in Moroccan Darija, there is no difference.

00:12:12.040 --> 00:12:13.640
In the Standard Arabic sentence,

00:12:13.649 --> 00:12:17.818
We see a different word "turiid" which is in the regular present tense form.

00:12:17.820 --> 00:12:24.520
This is the second person singular masculine and there's a different feminine form turiidiin(a) or turiidiin. next

00:12:24.580 --> 00:12:26.980
Next, we see the phrase for glass of water

00:12:26.999 --> 00:12:29.740
the main difference here lies in the prepositions.

00:12:29.740 --> 00:12:32.840
Standard Arabic uses "min", which means "from" and

00:12:32.920 --> 00:12:38.600
Darija uses "d" which I suspect comes from the French preposition "de".

00:12:38.780 --> 00:12:43.580
And one more sentence. This sentence means "yesterday, we ate chicken, but today we'll eat beef"

00:12:43.660 --> 00:12:47.540
"IbaraH klina djaj, wlakin lyum ghadi kanaklu lbgree."

00:12:47.540 --> 00:12:49.049
Word for word, it's "yesterday"

00:12:49.049 --> 00:12:52.859
"we ate", "chicken", "but", "today", future marker

00:12:52.860 --> 00:12:56.320
"we eat", "beef". And in Standard Arabic,

00:12:56.320 --> 00:13:01.040
"al-baraHa 'akalna al-dijaaj, wa lakin al-yawm sa-nakuul lahm al-baqar."

00:13:01.040 --> 00:13:04.320
First, we see basically the same words meaning yesterday.

00:13:04.320 --> 00:13:08.900
But Darija, the definite article is reduced and the final vowel is missing.

00:13:08.900 --> 00:13:13.780
There's another word for yesterday in Standard Arabic, "al-'ams", which is perhaps more common,

00:13:13.780 --> 00:13:16.920
but this one, "al-baraHa", is also Standard Arabic

00:13:16.980 --> 00:13:21.960
Next we see that Standard Arabic and Darija have very different forms of the same verb.

00:13:22.040 --> 00:13:28.620
Notice that the root letters "k", "l" are the same, but the Darija word is missing the first root letter "'aalif",

00:13:28.620 --> 00:13:31.020
and the vowel patterns are very different yet

00:13:31.020 --> 00:13:35.600
Both of these are the first-person plural past tense form of the verb "to eat".

00:13:35.600 --> 00:13:40.780
Here we see "wa lakin" is the same except that the vowels are shortened in Darija

00:13:40.780 --> 00:13:44.580
And here we have "lyum" which is "al- yawm" in Standard Arabic.

00:13:44.940 --> 00:13:53.380
Remember that in a previous sentence we saw "nhar" instead of "lyum" both are possible even though "nhar" tends to be common.

00:13:53.560 --> 00:13:55.560
Next we see the future tense.

00:13:55.560 --> 00:14:01.340
In Standard Arabic, the future tense is formed by adding the prefix "sa" to the present tense form, or

00:14:01.360 --> 00:14:04.280
alternatively another pre verb sawfa is used.

00:14:04.280 --> 00:14:08.840
But in Moroccan Darija, you add the pre verb "ghadi" to the present tense form.

00:14:08.840 --> 00:14:13.180
Notice again that the present tense form has the prefix "ka" and "naklu".

00:14:13.180 --> 00:14:16.020
This is equivalent to "nakuul" in Standard Arabic.

00:14:16.020 --> 00:14:19.580
But in Darija, the "uu" vowel here is shortened and we add a suffix "u"

00:14:19.580 --> 00:14:26.660
to indicate that this is first-person plural not first-person singular, since both of them have the prefix "n".

00:14:26.940 --> 00:14:31.700
In Darija, the first person singular resembles the first person plural in standard Arabic.

00:14:31.700 --> 00:14:35.240
So this vowel suffix is added to indicate plural.

00:14:35.240 --> 00:14:39.320
As you can see Moroccan Darija differs quite a lot from Standard Arabic.

00:14:39.320 --> 00:14:42.180
But is it different enough to be considered a different language?

00:14:42.180 --> 00:14:43.520
Well, that's debatable

00:14:43.520 --> 00:14:48.960
all Arabic dialects differ from Standard Arabic in ways similar to those seen in this video.

00:14:48.980 --> 00:14:51.440
The exact words and constructions aren't the same,

00:14:51.440 --> 00:14:55.180
but the same types of differences are prominent in most dialects.

00:14:55.180 --> 00:14:59.460
As Arabic expanded into new lands and was adopted by speakers of other languages

00:14:59.560 --> 00:15:03.680
well over 1200 years ago, new Arabic speakers in each community

00:15:03.720 --> 00:15:08.200
developed their own variety of Arabic that was influenced by their previous native language

00:15:08.280 --> 00:15:11.960
So while Moroccan Arabic shows the influence of Amazigh languages,

00:15:12.120 --> 00:15:15.580
Egyptian Arabic shows the influence of the Coptic language.

00:15:15.580 --> 00:15:19.960
And the dialects of the Levant region show the influence of Aramaic etc.

00:15:19.960 --> 00:15:22.820
From my point of view, the one thing that makes Moroccan Arabic

00:15:22.820 --> 00:15:25.220
less intelligible for speakers of other dialects

00:15:25.220 --> 00:15:29.520
is its phonology specifically its stress and the reduction of vowels.

00:15:29.520 --> 00:15:32.780
But it seems to me that this problem can be overcome through

00:15:32.780 --> 00:15:36.060
exposure and by the Moroccan speaker slowing down a little bit

00:15:36.060 --> 00:15:41.540
For an English speaker maybe it's comparable to say rural Scottish English spoken very quickly

00:15:41.540 --> 00:15:44.260
on first listen, it may sound to you like a different language.

00:15:44.260 --> 00:15:45.850
But if they slow down for you

00:15:45.850 --> 00:15:49.980
you might be able to understand it makes sense of all those different vowel sounds

00:15:49.980 --> 00:15:52.620
And assuming both of us have been to school and are literate

00:15:52.620 --> 00:15:56.040
We can adjust our speech a little bit to make it more like a book.

00:15:56.040 --> 00:16:01.900
And speakers of Moroccan Darija can do the same thing by including some elements of Standard Arabic into their speech

00:16:01.900 --> 00:16:05.540
or by using some elements of Syrian and Egyptian dialects.

